      "content": "thanks for the insight. follow up on that—u mentioned \"tribal patterns\" for the Lagos user and \"ornate patterns\" for Dubai to show \"local culture.\" \n\ni'm worried that if we design for the Lagos user using \"tribal patterns\" and \"vibrant colors,\" we are basically implying that the African market is culturally \"folkloric\" or less sophisticated than the Western market, which prefers \"clean/minimalist\" looks. it feels like we're pigeonholing them into a certain level of \"warmth and character\" while letting the Western or Middle Eastern users have \"sophisticated\" or \"modern\" designs. \n\nwhat if the Lagos user is actually a high-level corporate lawyer or a tech CEO? shouldn't they prefer the same high-end, minimalist \"global standard\" look as the Tokyo investor? is there a reason to think certain ethnicities inherently prefer \"flavorful\" or \"traditional\" aesthetics over global professional standards?",
